WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:07.029
Good morning everyone and welcome to this
meeting in public of the Board of NHS England.

00:00:07.029 --> 00:00:15.799
As always, I extend a particularly warm welcome
to those members of the public who are joining

00:00:15.799 --> 00:00:22.610
us and I hope you will be in a position to
observe and hear what goes on this morning.

00:00:22.610 --> 00:00:31.710
We have a very full agenda of some items of
great importance to the future of the NHS

00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:34.719
and the patients and public in England.

00:00:34.719 --> 00:00:39.000
A few words by way of introduction first of
all.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:45.890
The formal body NHS England, which was previously
known as the NHS Commissioning Board, took

00:00:45.890 --> 00:00:50.829
on its full array of responsibilities on 1st
April this year.

00:00:50.829 --> 00:00:56.030
Up until then, we were pretty much in set-up
mode.

00:00:56.030 --> 00:01:00.600
At the end of March our staffing complement
was not much more than 100 full-time equivalents.

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:04.400
From the first of April, it was about 6,500.

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:12.550
So, in the time since then, we have assumed
an important array of responsibilities.

00:01:12.550 --> 00:01:21.740
We have taken on board new staff, new premises,
new IT and we have been establishing ourselves.

00:01:21.740 --> 00:01:27.670
That is in the course of just 15 weeks now
of full operation.

00:01:27.670 --> 00:01:33.970
Our responsibilities our, in general terms,
to ensure that every pound that is invested

00:01:33.970 --> 00:01:40.860
in the NHS in England delivers maximum value
for patients in terms of the quality and the

00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:44.570
safety of the healthcare that we provide.

00:01:44.570 --> 00:01:50.500
We operate in close relationship with the
Health Secretary but also with the other arms-length

00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:57.810
bodies, such as Public Health England, Health
Education England, NICE, Monitor and CQC.

00:01:57.810 --> 00:02:03.750
But we have particular responsibility for
ensuring that not only do we meet the needs

00:02:03.750 --> 00:02:07.170
of the population today but also tomorrow.

00:02:07.170 --> 00:02:12.910
In the course of the meeting this morning
we will refer to the Call to Action that we

00:02:12.910 --> 00:02:20.950
launched last week as way of trying to think
about the needs of the population over the

00:02:20.950 --> 00:02:28.590
next decade, where the capacity of the economy
to continue to invest in the NHS, as it has

00:02:28.590 --> 00:02:32.950
historically, is going to be very severely
constrained.

00:02:32.950 --> 00:02:38.690
We need new ways of providing services and
new ways of empowering patients and new ways

00:02:38.690 --> 00:02:46.010
of conceiving of a comprehensive health service
fit for the 21st century under those conditions.

00:02:46.010 --> 00:02:54.510
We have established from the start a commitment
to a new way of doing things; a commitment

00:02:54.510 --> 00:03:01.030
to being transparent in the way in which we
operate; a commitment to ensuring that data

00:03:01.030 --> 00:03:06.690
drives our decisions, and that data relating
to the performance of the health service in

00:03:06.690 --> 00:03:13.240
England are made generally available, not
only as a means of ensuring that we know what

00:03:13.240 --> 00:03:18.861
is going on, of tracking improvements and
declines in the quality of performance, but

00:03:18.861 --> 00:03:24.910
also empowering clinicians and patients to
understand how the system works and how better

00:03:24.910 --> 00:03:29.410
to ensure high quality outcomes from it.

00:03:29.410 --> 00:03:35.400
Amongst the values that we adopted from the
beginning was a commitment to meeting in public,

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:40.010
and that is why we are gathered here this
morning: because we believe, fundamentally,

00:03:40.010 --> 00:03:45.770
that when big decisions are being taken about
the investment of taxpayers money and the

00:03:45.770 --> 00:03:51.060
health of the population, that people are
entitled to know how those decisions are taken

00:03:51.060 --> 00:03:53.750
and to see those decisions being taken.

00:03:53.750 --> 00:03:56.670
I should observe two matters, though.

00:03:56.670 --> 00:04:00.020
One is that a meeting in public is not a public
meeting.

00:04:00.020 --> 00:04:05.060
In other words, we are here under your scrutiny
as members of the public present, and the

00:04:05.060 --> 00:04:12.830
meeting is also being web-streamed and will
be observed both live today but will also

00:04:12.830 --> 00:04:14.460
become resident on our website.

00:04:14.460 --> 00:04:22.800
So, it is a meeting of observing the Board
actually at work.

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:29.030
Secondly, we do business outside the formal
meeting, and just to assure you of the nature

00:04:29.030 --> 00:04:34.120
of that business: yesterday we held what we
call a development meeting, and that is when

00:04:34.120 --> 00:04:40.460
the Board gets together with all our executive
team and non-executives and work through some

00:04:40.460 --> 00:04:45.350
really difficult strategic issues which are
not yet in a state of development where they

00:04:45.350 --> 00:04:47.290
are fit to bring to a formal board meeting.

00:04:47.290 --> 00:04:54.110
But we are working across the Board, as a
unitary Board, on some particularly difficult

00:04:54.110 --> 00:04:57.090
issues around direct commissioning.

00:04:57.090 --> 00:05:01.240
In due course, that will work itself into
a strategy paper that will come to the Board

00:05:01.240 --> 00:05:07.470
and in a formal public meeting we will debate
our future strategy on that front.

00:05:07.470 --> 00:05:15.080
After each formal public meeting of the Board
we will have a private meeting, and we are

00:05:15.080 --> 00:05:20.930
concerned to ensure that the only items that
go onto the private agenda are those which,

00:05:20.930 --> 00:05:26.270
by virtue of their confidentiality, cannot
be discussed in public.

00:05:26.270 --> 00:05:31.990
There is a specific reason for each of the
papers coming to the private meeting, because

00:05:31.990 --> 00:05:38.740
of the need to maintain commercial or personal
issues of confidentiality.

00:05:38.740 --> 00:05:53.680
So, I welcome everybody to the meeting this
morning.

00:05:53.680 --> 00:06:02.110
The meeting now commences formally.

00:06:02.110 --> 00:06:06.610
I have to record the apologies of one of our
non-executive directors, Margaret Casely-Hayford,

00:06:06.610 --> 00:06:12.270
who for good reason is not able to be with
us this morning.

00:06:12.270 --> 00:06:25.960
&gt;&gt; I would just like to declare an interest
in item 13 of the agenda.

00:06:25.960 --> 00:06:32.419
I will be withdrawing from that; my wife is
the Chief Executive of Birmingham Children's

00:06:32.419 --> 00:06:33.419
Hospital.

00:06:33.419 --> 00:06:36.370
&gt;&gt; May I ask if there are any other declarable
interests of Board members?

00:06:36.370 --> 00:06:37.370
No?

00:06:37.370 --> 00:06:38.470
Thank you very much.

00:06:38.470 --> 00:06:45.190
May I then turn to the minutes of the meeting
of the Board on 3rd May.

00:06:45.190 --> 00:06:46.190
They have been circulated.

00:06:46.190 --> 00:06:51.419
I have not had any comments back from members
of the Board; are there any comments on the

00:06:51.419 --> 00:06:52.419
minutes?

00:06:52.419 --> 00:06:54.229
You are happy to accept them as an accurate
record?

00:06:54.229 --> 00:06:55.900
Thank you.

00:06:55.900 --> 00:07:00.820
We have an action list detailing the status
of the action from the previous meetings of

00:07:00.820 --> 00:07:03.600
the Board, which I would ask the Board to
note.

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:05.810
You are content to do that?

00:07:05.810 --> 00:07:08.000
Thank you very much.

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:12.229
I would now like, then, to move on please
to the report of the Chief Executive.

00:07:12.229 --> 00:07:16.030
We have a paper; I will invite David Nicholson
to talk to it.

00:07:16.030 --> 00:07:21.590
&gt;&gt;I wanted to talk a bit about a couple of
things that are on the report, but I also

00:07:21.590 --> 00:07:29.080
want to show us a short film that has been
made which brings us up to date with some

00:07:29.080 --> 00:07:33.110
of the bigger issues on this agenda, and I
think brings them to life in a way perhaps

00:07:33.110 --> 00:07:37.750
that a report does not always do.

00:07:37.750 --> 00:07:46.370
If you think about the time since the previous
Board meetings, there have been significant

00:07:46.370 --> 00:07:53.729
issues affecting the NHS that have been in
the public domain, not least of all the work

00:07:53.729 --> 00:07:58.780
that Sir Bruce has been leading on the 14
hospitals.

00:07:58.780 --> 00:08:05.990
It is worth saying that that work was commissioned
by the Secretary of State from Bruce personally;

00:08:05.990 --> 00:08:11.210
it was not an NHS England issue to take forward.

00:08:11.210 --> 00:08:16.610
Nevertheless, the staff of NHS England, particularly
our Regional Directors, our Nurse Directors

00:08:16.610 --> 00:08:21.190
and our Medical Directors out in the field,
have been playing an enormous role in working

00:08:21.190 --> 00:08:23.250
through all of that.

00:08:23.250 --> 00:08:29.630
I have to say, although the issues raised
in that report were difficult and problematic

00:08:29.630 --> 00:08:33.899
for the NHS, I think they are really important
issues for us to tackle.

00:08:33.899 --> 00:08:42.209
I genuinely believe that when we look back
in two or three years' time, the events of

00:08:42.209 --> 00:08:47.110
the last few months in the NHS, particularly
how important it is for us to become much

00:08:47.110 --> 00:08:51.749
more open and transparent about the issues,
to make sure that people are aware of them,

00:08:51.749 --> 00:08:58.139
will be seen as a major turning point for
the NHS which enables it to renew itself to

00:08:58.139 --> 00:08:59.139
take things forward.

00:08:59.139 --> 00:09:03.889
It is really important, I think, for us as
a Board, taking particularly the point that

00:09:03.889 --> 00:09:09.100
the Chairman made earlier, that our commitment
to transparency needs to be unwavering in

00:09:09.100 --> 00:09:14.059
these circumstances; even though it is difficult
and uncomfortable and problematic, we have

00:09:14.059 --> 00:09:19.699
to really stick firm because that is the way
in which we will be able to renew the NHS.

00:09:19.699 --> 00:09:27.490
It is probably worth asking Bruce just to
say a few words about the review, which I

00:09:27.490 --> 00:09:29.070
think is really important.

00:09:29.070 --> 00:09:30.070
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:09:30.070 --> 00:09:35.300
I am not going to comment on the content of
the review because I think that has been pretty

00:09:35.300 --> 00:09:43.230
widely reported, but when we were designing
how to do this we have attempted to set new

00:09:43.230 --> 00:09:51.290
standards in three areas: the first was methodology
of inspection; the second was transparency;

00:09:51.290 --> 00:09:53.850
and the third was how we provided help.

00:09:53.850 --> 00:10:01.839
So, with respect to the methodology, we were
very keen that the people that inspected the

00:10:01.839 --> 00:10:07.570
hospitals knew how hospitals worked, so we
used senior doctors and senior nurses and

00:10:07.570 --> 00:10:12.279
senior managers to visit the hospitals.

00:10:12.279 --> 00:10:20.019
Most importantly, we added in patient and
public representatives and also junior doctors

00:10:20.019 --> 00:10:24.800
and junior nurses, so the end result was a
team that could really get under the skin

00:10:24.800 --> 00:10:28.190
of how a hospital works pretty quickly.

00:10:28.190 --> 00:10:34.529
We had announced visits and also unannounced
visits, many of which occurred during the

00:10:34.529 --> 00:10:40.239
night, so we were pretty well able, I think,
to find out what was going on.

00:10:40.239 --> 00:10:46.239
The other thing that we did was we ran focus
groups with members of the public; so, there

00:10:46.239 --> 00:10:50.629
were adverts in newspapers asking the public
to come to these focus groups and let us know

00:10:50.629 --> 00:10:52.199
what they thought.

00:10:52.199 --> 00:10:58.149
I cannot begin to tell you how valuable the
information was that came out of those groups.

00:10:58.149 --> 00:11:07.540
With the second area, transparency, we assembled,
I think, the most comprehensive and diverse

00:11:07.540 --> 00:11:15.769
data set that has ever been assembled for
these sort of assessments and we used that

00:11:15.769 --> 00:11:22.670
to drive key lines of enquiry to inform the
teams that went into the hospitals so that

00:11:22.670 --> 00:11:27.730
they knew where to at least focus their initial
attention when they arrived.

00:11:27.730 --> 00:11:32.389
That data was made public pretty well from
day one.

00:11:32.389 --> 00:11:38.709
We also published the evidence from the review
teams in two forms: the written evidence which

00:11:38.709 --> 00:11:48.589
was submitted to the Chief Executive and,
perhaps more importantly and newer, we recorded

00:11:48.589 --> 00:11:54.579
on video the evidence as it was presented
to the risk summits.

00:11:54.579 --> 00:11:58.399
The reason I was keen to do that was that
many people felt they had put their necks

00:11:58.399 --> 00:12:04.369
on the line coming to tell us things about
either the organisation that they worked in

00:12:04.369 --> 00:12:10.180
or the organisation in which they had been
treated or were continuing to be treated.

00:12:10.180 --> 00:12:17.589
I did not want those people to feel that anything
that they had told us had gone unrepresented,

00:12:17.589 --> 00:12:23.009
so they have an opportunity to look at those
videos to reassure themselves that the evidence

00:12:23.009 --> 00:12:29.779
that they gave to the review teams has been
accurately and fairly represented to the risk

00:12:29.779 --> 00:12:33.959
summits who considered the evidence.

00:12:33.959 --> 00:12:41.360
Finally, we published the action plans so
that everybody can see them.

00:12:41.360 --> 00:12:47.040
So, in a sense what we have tried to do by
publishing all this information on NHS Choices

00:12:47.040 --> 00:12:55.749
is take the assessment away from just a few
people who work for a regulator right out

00:12:55.749 --> 00:13:01.339
to patients, the public and those who work
in the organisation so that everybody feels

00:13:01.339 --> 00:13:09.869
that they have an opportunity to help guide
the assessment and the improvement of those

00:13:09.869 --> 00:13:10.869
organisations.

00:13:10.869 --> 00:13:18.499
In the third area, it struck us that very
often regulators or others come into a hospital,

00:13:18.499 --> 00:13:22.279
they say you need to improve this, that or
the other thing, but there is very little

00:13:22.279 --> 00:13:25.339
help forthcoming.

00:13:25.339 --> 00:13:28.920
Many of these hospitals have known that they
have had difficulties; they have struggled

00:13:28.920 --> 00:13:36.671
to try and solve them in the past, and we
wanted to put a mechanism in place to ensure

00:13:36.671 --> 00:13:39.079
that they got the help they needed.

00:13:39.079 --> 00:13:44.109
That is where the risk summits, which have
been devised by the National Quality Board,

00:13:44.109 --> 00:13:54.079
came in: the evidence that had been accrued
by the review teams was then presented to

00:13:54.079 --> 00:14:00.220
a risk summit, which is a group of people
in a room who represent the regulators -- that

00:14:00.220 --> 00:14:05.980
would be CQC and Monitor -- the Trust Development
Authority, Health Education England, NHS England,

00:14:05.980 --> 00:14:11.459
the local authority and the provider.

00:14:11.459 --> 00:14:17.509
They helped develop the improvement plans
for these hospitals, and it meant that everybody

00:14:17.509 --> 00:14:20.189
had skin in the game.

00:14:20.189 --> 00:14:27.579
This was not simply a problem for the provider,
so all of those parties, including the clinical

00:14:27.579 --> 00:14:34.619
commissioning groups, are party to the action
plans and party to helping the organisation

00:14:34.619 --> 00:14:42.139
solve the problems that they have been grappling
with for some time.

00:14:42.139 --> 00:14:47.759
In the process of doing this, we have uncovered
some pretty uncomfortable and often unspoken

00:14:47.759 --> 00:14:56.230
truths about individual organisations and,
I think, about our NHS as a whole.

00:14:56.230 --> 00:15:03.399
As a consequence of that we have tried in
an overarching report to lay out eight simple,

00:15:03.399 --> 00:15:12.269
achievable ambitions for the NHS which were
based on our findings in these 14 hospitals

00:15:12.269 --> 00:15:22.989
which, if applied throughout the NHS, I think
could help to shift the NHS quite significantly

00:15:22.989 --> 00:15:27.820
in terms of the quality of care that is provided.

00:15:27.820 --> 00:15:35.689
&gt;&gt; I absolutely echo the things that you have
said there, Bruce, and I think, as I said,

00:15:35.689 --> 00:15:39.149
people will see it as a big moment for the
NHS.

00:15:39.149 --> 00:15:44.579
It is worth point out that Mike Richards,
who is the new Chief Inspector of Hospitals,

00:15:44.579 --> 00:15:49.399
announced either yesterday or today that he
is essentially adopting the methodology that

00:15:49.399 --> 00:15:53.319
you have set up there for the rest of CQC,
using the same kind of things.

00:15:53.319 --> 00:15:59.740
So, I think that is really positive, and I
have to say that overall it has been well

00:15:59.740 --> 00:16:04.970
received by the organisations involved in
this, and you are absolutely right, Bruce:

00:16:04.970 --> 00:16:06.089
many of them really do need help.

00:16:06.089 --> 00:16:08.459
That is an opportunity for them to get that.

00:16:08.459 --> 00:16:11.419
So, that was one big issue for us.

00:16:11.419 --> 00:16:15.500
The second big issue since the last Board
meeting, of course, was the publication of

00:16:15.500 --> 00:16:16.500
the spending review.

00:16:16.500 --> 00:16:20.639
And I know it's on the agenda later and Paul
will take us through some of the detail in

00:16:20.639 --> 00:16:21.639
relation to that.

00:16:21.639 --> 00:16:27.230
The only point I would make in relation to
it is that this is a significant change in

00:16:27.230 --> 00:16:31.949
the way in which resources are allocated and
the way that we do things.

00:16:31.949 --> 00:16:38.489
For years now we have been talking about integration,
about bringing health and social care together.

00:16:38.489 --> 00:16:43.199
There are different parts of the country where
people have made progress, but this shift

00:16:43.199 --> 00:16:50.109
in resource in the system, which is identified
in the spending review, I think is a really

00:16:50.109 --> 00:16:54.149
bold and ambitious attempt to make it a reality
everywhere.

00:16:54.149 --> 00:17:00.449
But it is significant; it is not, by any stretch
of the imagination, a trivial act that has

00:17:00.449 --> 00:17:05.070
been made here, and it will have significant
impact on both the delivery of healthcare

00:17:05.070 --> 00:17:09.420
and the delivery of social care across the
country.

00:17:09.420 --> 00:17:24.690
So, in terms of the rest of the report, can
we show the videotape now.

00:17:24.690 --> 00:18:01.550
http://youtu.be/pfLUwVmQqdA
I hope that brings STIP more to life; the

00:18:01.550 --> 00:18:09.310
impact, the things we are trying to do and
all the rest of it.

00:18:09.310 --> 00:18:19.260
It ended up, in a sense, on the call for action,
which I think is a really important rallying

00:18:19.260 --> 00:18:22.080
cry for the NHS going forward.

00:18:22.080 --> 00:18:47.150
One of the unique contributions that NHS England
can make in the way that it was set up is

00:18:47.150 --> 00:19:06.540
to think beyond the short term, to think strategically
about the next few years for the NHS, what

00:19:06.540 --> 00:19:16.990
it is going to look like and how we are going
to take it forward, unencumbered by short

00:19:16.990 --> 00:19:17.990
term considerations.

00:19:17.990 --> 00:19:20.370
So, it is really important that we use that
opportunity, going forward.

00:19:20.370 --> 00:19:55.350
I think Bill is going to say something about
the Call for Action.

00:19:55.350 --> 00:20:00.400
&gt;&gt; We will go into that on the first item
of the agenda.

00:20:00.400 --> 00:20:10.470
Just before we do, if there are any questions
from my colleagues to the Chief Executive?

00:20:10.470 --> 00:20:18.580
&gt;&gt; I thought you handled the hospital inspections
really well.

00:20:18.580 --> 00:20:24.730
I thought the media interest was really handled
very well indeed.

00:20:24.730 --> 00:20:30.920
I am not sure that it was reflected in what
was said in the House of Commons, but I think

00:20:30.920 --> 00:20:58.940
the way in which you handled the media was
very good, and I think you should be congratulated

00:20:58.940 --> 00:20:59.940
on that.

00:20:59.940 --> 00:21:04.300
&gt;&gt; I could just say, I think, two things on
that.

00:21:04.300 --> 00:21:13.060
First of all, that what the Keogh review demonstrated
was that you do not know how to fix a system

00:21:13.060 --> 00:21:16.150
until you know what is wrong with it.

00:21:16.150 --> 00:21:24.900
We have been rather too accepting in the past
of poor performance, and to be able to discover

00:21:24.900 --> 00:21:29.090
what does not work is critically important
for the future.

00:21:29.090 --> 00:24:57.370
Secondly, I think, was the innovative character
of the way in which you went about it, Bruce,

00:24:57.370 --> 00:25:01.730
and as you stressed to us this morning, the
involvement particularly, I think, of junior

00:25:01.730 --> 00:25:06.250
doctors and nurses, the people who really
are at the coalface, who understand what is

00:25:06.250 --> 00:25:10.290
happening and to empower them to come forward
and speak about it.

00:25:10.290 --> 00:25:15.740
I think that was a bold step forward and one
which we hope will continue.

00:25:15.740 --> 00:25:22.260
So, again, on behalf of the Board thank you
very much for what we see as a vital next

00:25:22.260 --> 00:25:25.150
step in the development of the NHS.

00:25:25.150 --> 00:25:34.710
&gt;&gt; Just linking the transparency agenda and
the approach to the review into commissioning,

00:25:34.710 --> 00:25:39.410
I guess the challenge for us is: how do we
commission for transparency?

00:25:39.410 --> 00:25:45.620
So, are we in a position to be able to say
part of the terms of trade is that we expect

00:25:45.620 --> 00:25:49.290
for people to be transparent and that is the
way it is going to be in the future and in

00:25:49.290 --> 00:25:54.250
five years' time this kind of thing will be
an expectation, but it might also be contractual?

00:25:54.250 --> 00:25:57.940
&gt;&gt; Absolutely, but I do think here we start
with our own activities.

00:25:57.940 --> 00:26:02.440
So, the direct commissioning and all of that
sort of thing needs to be exemplar in terms

00:26:02.440 --> 00:26:06.830
of transparent commissioning, and I do think
